  "ai_summary": "Great Britain's wind fleet is currently generating 14.3 GW, with Scotland contributing an estimated 5.2 GW, representing 51.5% of installed Scottish wind capacity. This robust renewable output drives Scottish carbon intensity down to an exceptionally clean 2 gCO2/kWh, compared to 61 gCO2/kWh GB-wide. However, severe cross-border transmission bottlenecks continue to strand zero-carbon electricity in the north. As a direct result, 22.5 GWh of Scottish generation has been curtailed so far today through Balancing Mechanism bids, incurring a direct constraint cost of about \u00a31,159,831. To replace this trapped power closer to southern demand centers, fossil-fueled generators elsewhere have burned an estimated 4,276,824 m3 of gas, emitting an avoidable 7,878 tonnes of CO2 into the atmosphere. Extrapolating today's constraint expenses produces an estimated total yearly cost of curtailment of \u00a3423 million. Spread across national electricity consumption, this structural grid bottleneck increases everybody's invoice in the UK by an estimated \u00a31.5/MWh, underlining the critical financial and environmental necessity of accelerating transmission infrastructure reinforcement."
